@bartonjs It looks like `Initialize` is a no-op on .NET (Core). We could do any of the following:

1. Implement on the `HashProvider`. For some platforms that support a reset like `EVP_DigestInit_ex` we could use that, or, more simply, just dispose the underlying handles and re-initialize the whole thing.
2. `[Obsolete]` this method and document it as so, and expect that callers should either re-create the hash instance themselves, or finalize the instance and discard the result to get a reset.

`TransformFinalBlock` also does a reset.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;

using var hashAlg = SHA1.Create();

byte[] input1 = new byte[] { 1, 2, 3 }, input2 = new byte[] { 4, 5, 6 };
byte[] dest1 = new byte[3];

hashAlg.TransformBlock(input1, 0, input1.Length, dest1, 0);
byte[] digest = hashAlg.TransformFinalBlock(input2, 0, input2.Length);

Console.WriteLine(Convert.ToBase64String(digest));


hashAlg.TransformBlock(input1, 0, input1.Length, dest1, 0);
digest = hashAlg.TransformFinalBlock(input2, 0, input2.Length);

Console.WriteLine(Convert.ToBase64String(digest));
```

This will output the same hash even though we are re-using an instance, so we can observe that the first call to `TransformFinalBlock` re-initialized. The only time you would want to call `Initialize` explicitly is when you are updating a hash with `TransformBlock` and you never get the final digest, but want to re-use the instance anyway.

-------

Some other thoughts that are not explicitly related to this, but might be helpful to you:

1. > For small data packets and small files the ComputeHash is used.

    Since it looks like you are targeting .NET 5, a better option might be for you to use the one-shot static methods. Like [`SHA256.HashData`][1]. Since these methods are static, there are no object instances that you need to pool. They will oftentimes be faster as well.

2. You might consider using [`IncrementalHash`][2] for "updating" hashes for your scenario of `TransformBlock` and `TransformFinalBlock`. The `TransformBlock` APIs exist so that `HashAlgorithm` instances could be used with `CryptoStream`. If you are just hashing inputs then `IncrementalHash` might make more sense. The `TransformBlock` APIs unnecessarily copy the input buffer to the output buffer. If you _are_ hashing a stream, then consider using `HashAlgorithm.ComputeHash` which takes a `Stream` as input. 

[1]:  https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.security.cryptography.sha256.hashdata?view=net-5.0
[2]: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.security.cryptography.sha256.hashdata?view=net-5.0
